<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ryūnosuke Akutagawa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1892">1892</a>–<a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Akutagawa Ryūnosuke, Chōkōdō Shujin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ryūnosuke Akutagawa was a Japanese writer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taisho-period">Taishō period</a>, born in Tokyo in 1892, regarded as the father of the Japanese short story. Raised by the family of his maternal uncle, from whom he took the name Akutagawa, he studied English literature at Tokyo Imperial University and won fame with Hana (The Nose, 1916), which Natsume Sōseki praised. Many of his 150 short stories reinterpret classical tales, among them Rashōmon and Yabu no naka (In a Grove, 1922), which Akira Kurosawa retold in his 1950 film Rashōmon. After years of hallucinations and the fear that he had inherited his mother's mental disorder, he took his own life in 1927 at 35; the Akutagawa Prize is named after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ryunosuke-akutagawa.jpg" alt="Ryūnosuke Akutagawa" width="330" height="478" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Akutagawa_portrait_Akutagawa_Sakuhinshu_6.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Akutagawa was the eldest son of the businessman Toshizō Niihara, who founded a successful dairy business. The Akutagawa family belonged to the warrior class and had served the Tokugawa clan as okubōzu, who managed the tearoom and served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>; they looked down on his father as a parvenu. Their household enjoyed art, and he took an early interest in classical Chinese literature and in the works of Mori Ōgai and Natsume Sōseki.</p><p>He entered the First Higher School in 1910 and made friends with Kan Kikuchi, Masao Kume and others who also became authors. He began writing at Tokyo Imperial University, where he studied English literature from 1913. He married Fumi Tsukamoto in 1918, and they had three sons, among them the actor Hiroshi and the composer Yasushi. After graduating he taught briefly at the Naval Engineering School in Yokosuka.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1914 Akutagawa and his school friends revived the journal Shinshichō, in which they published translations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-b-yeats">William Butler Yeats</a> and Anatole France. His story Rashōmon, based on a twelfth-century tale, appeared in 1915 and was harshly judged by his friends. A series of stories followed set in the Heian, Edo and early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-era">Meiji periods</a>, reinterpreting classical works, often from the Konjaku Monogatarishū, among them Jigokuhen (Hell Screen, 1918). He strongly opposed naturalism.</p><p>In 1921 he spent four months in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> as a reporter for the Osaka Mainichi Shinbun, visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Suzhou</a>. The trip was stressful, and his health never recovered. His work held that literature should be universal and could join Western and Japanese cultures.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His final works included Kappa, a satire based on the creature of Japanese folklore, and Haguruma, both of 1927. He died on 24 July 1927 by an overdose of barbital, and left a note to Masao Kume speaking of a vague sense of unease about his future.</p><p>Kurosawa's film Rashōmon takes its title and its frame scenes at the gate from Akutagawa's story and its plot from In a Grove. Its central conceit, conflicting accounts of one event with none definitive, has become an accepted device of storytelling. In 1935 Kan Kikuchi founded the Akutagawa Prize in his honour.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ryūnosuke Akutagawa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
